Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox Unreleased --- Current User-Visible Changes

** Optional fallback tuning
Choose Customize this voice from an editor fallback row to set individual
adjustments, use the shared value, or restore the adapter default. Automatic
samples audition that row; the same voice draft and Save action own all changes.


Released notes are preserved in [[file:NEWS-2026.9.4][NEWS for Emacsvox 2026.9.4]].

** Tracked speech handles early playback replies

Tracked reading now retains playback completion received while a speech request
is still being written. Stopping speech cancels pending callbacks, and failed
submissions cannot restore cancelled tracking or replay the request. Speech
started by playback callbacks gets its own submission.

Stop also cancels speech still being prepared, including a request interrupted
during its automatic stop of older speech. A newer urgent announcement can
supersede that request. Interrupted preparation releases its tracking slots.

** Voice editor gives spoken adjustment feedback

Automatic samples now start with the new adjustment value. Numeric controls
stop at their limits and announce minimum or maximum, and the automatic-sample
toggle announces on or off. Arrow and Tab navigation stop at the first and
last fields without wrapping around the editor.

Complete voice comparisons and individual auditions now share cancellation
handling. Stop and newer previews retire the previous sample's pending work;
late replies cannot start another comparison entry. Individual auditions also
have a playback timeout. A failed or interrupted write is never replayed.

With Omnivox per-choice tuning support, editor previews now preserve individual
fallback settings and contextual overrides. A row can be auditioned or compared
with its original after reordering. Playback feedback distinguishes the sample
from spoken comparison labels and audio that was accepted but never started.
Text changes, undo, save and context recapture retire stale previews. Earlier
playback evidence cannot replace a newer view's result.

** Omnivox 1.9.0 minimum for voice previews

Omnivox 1.9.0 or newer is now the supported speech-server baseline for complete
voice previews. The guided Windows and WSL installers pin 1.9.0, together with
matching optional engine modules, and Debian packages recommend that minimum.

Upgrade an existing Omnivox installation separately. Updating Emacsvox does
not replace your selected server or install optional engine modules.

** Voice palette selections apply consistently

Search and incremental-search hits now use the active palette's bolden voice.
Nonincremental searches, including regexp and history-repeat commands, speak
the destination line and cue a successful hit once.

Completion, status speech, spelling feedback, widgets, and other integrations
now retain named voices instead of freezing legacy ACSS presets. Calendar
marked dates and CPerl/table indentation use the normal speech path, and the
named-voice sampler follows the active palette. Voice Lock and explicit aural
rules retain their existing control over formatting voices.

Restart Emacs after updating to pick up the new voice defaults. Explicit
personal voice assignments are preserved; use names such as ='voice-bolden=
when those assignments should follow the palette. Legacy =voice-*-settings=
remain the fallback for voices without custom palette definitions.

** Responsive shell completion under WSL

Shell command-name completion now skips Windows drive directories by default,
avoiding long pauses scanning directories such as System32. Windows programs
still run by name, and explicit Windows filename completion remains available.
Enable =emacsvox-shell-complete-windows-commands= to restore those command-name
candidates. Native Windows and remote Shell buffers are unchanged.
